      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sol kadhi is drunk after fish meals along the Konkan coast, from the port towns of Maharashtra down to Goa. It is considered a digestive that helps the body after a rich seafood meal. Kokum, the traditional souring agent, grows only on the Konkan coast and in parts of Karnataka, and its sharp, slightly sweet flavour is distinctive. Sol kadhi is also made in Goa using the same method and is popular in coastal restaurants year-round."
          }
        ]
      }
